{
  "analysis": "The binary prints: 'Creating high-resolution image: 800x600', 'This will be downsampled with ffmpeg after rendering', 'Rendering high-resolution image...', 'Progress: 99.8%', 'Rendering complete'. The 'Progress:' likely uses carriage returns to update. There's no ffmpeg actually invoked (just a message). I need to determine exact output, whether any file is produced, and the precise progress behavior. 'file' command is unavailable. Let me check the precise output bytes and look at the binary's strings/disassembly.",
  "plan": "Step 1: Capture exact output with od/cat -A to see carriage returns and exact formatting. Check for any output files produced. Step 2: Examine strings and disassembly to reconstruct logic. I'll emit progress markers. Let me run it redirecting to a file and inspect, plus list files after.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la > /tmp/before.txt && ./mystery > /tmp/out.txt 2>&1; echo \"EXIT=$?\"; ls -la > /tmp/after.txt; diff /tmp/before.txt /tmp/after.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat -A /tmp/out.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "wc -c /tmp/out.txt\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Step 1 ANALYSIS: The program prints fixed messages then a carriage-return progress bar. The progress values are 0.0%..99.8% stepping in a pattern. I worked out the pattern: increments in tenths-of-percent are 2,1,2,2,1,2,2,1... which exactly matches a loop `for i=0..N-1 printf(\"\\rProgress: %.1f%%\", 100.0*i/N)` with N=600 and standard `%.1f` rounding. i=599 gives 100*599/600=99.833 -> 99.8%. So N=600 = image height. The program also writes image.ppm. I must reproduce the EXACT stdout AND the PPM file content. I need to decompile to learn the pixel-generation algorithm.",
  "plan": "Step 2: Examine the generated image.ppm (size, header, pixel pattern) and check available reverse-engineering tools (objdump, strings, readelf, gdb). Then disassemble main to recover the pixel formula. Emit progress markers as I advance.",
  "commands": [
